#: ../../../globbing.rst:2
msgid "Globbing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:4
msgid ""
"Xonsh supports four forms of filename globbing: normal (shell-style) "
"globbing, regular expression globbing, match globbing, and formatted glob "
"literals. All can be used in both subprocess mode and Python mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:10
msgid "Normal Globbing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:12
msgid ""
"Filename globbing with the ``*`` character is allowed in subprocess mode. "
"This uses Python's ``glob`` module under the covers. As an example, start "
"with a directory of files:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:16
msgid ""
"@ mkdir -p test/a/b\n"
"@ touch test/f.txt test/a/f.txt test/a/b/f.txt\n"
"@ ls test\n"
"a  f.txt"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:23
msgid ""
"In subprocess mode, normal globbing happens without any special syntax. "
"However, there is backtick syntax that is available inside Python mode as "
"well as subprocess mode. This can be done using ``g``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:27
msgid ""
"@ ls test/*.txt\n"
"test/f.txt\n"
"@ print(g`test/*.txt`)\n"
"['test/f.txt']"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:34
msgid ""
"The ``*`` wildcard matches any characters within a single path component. "
"The ``?`` wildcard matches a single character. The ``[abc]`` syntax matches "
"one of the enclosed characters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:38
msgid ""
"Recursive globbing with ``**`` matches zero or more intermediate "
"directories. For example, ``test/**/f.txt`` matches ``test/f.txt``, ``test/a/"
"f.txt``, and ``test/a/b/f.txt``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:42
msgid ""
"@ print(g`test/**/f.txt`)\n"
"['test/a/b/f.txt', 'test/a/f.txt', 'test/f.txt']"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:49
msgid "Hidden (Dot) Files"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:51
msgid ""
"By default, globbing excludes hidden files and directories — those whose "
"names begin with a literal ``.``. The ``$DOTGLOB`` environment variable "
"controls this for all globbing forms: normal globs (``g``), regex globs "
"(``r``), and bare subprocess wildcards."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:56
msgid ""
"When ``$DOTGLOB`` is ``False`` (the default), dotfiles are filtered out:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:58
msgid ""
"@ touch visible .hidden\n"
"@ print(g`*`)\n"
"['visible']"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:64
msgid "When ``$DOTGLOB`` is ``True``, dotfiles are included:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:66
msgid ""
"@ $DOTGLOB = True\n"
"@ print(g`*`)\n"
"['.hidden', 'visible']"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:72
msgid "The same applies to regex globs:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:74
msgid ""
"@ $DOTGLOB = True\n"
"@ print(`\\..*`)\n"
"['.hidden']"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:82
msgid "Regular Expression Globbing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:84
msgid ""
"If you have ever felt that normal globbing could use some more octane, then "
"regex globbing is the tool for you! Any string that uses backticks (`````) "
"instead of quotes (``'``, ``\"``) is interpreted as a regular expression to "
"match filenames against. Like with regular globbing, a list of successful "
"matches is returned. In Python mode, this is just a list of strings. In "
"subprocess mode, each filename becomes its own argument to the subprocess "
"command."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:92
msgid ""
"This same kind of search is performed if the backticks are prefaced with "
"``r``. So the following expressions are equivalent: ````test```` and "
"``r`test```."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:95
msgid ""
"@ touch a aa aaa aba abba aab aabb abcba\n"
"@ ls `a(a+|b+)a`\n"
"aaa  aba  abba\n"
"@ print(`a(a+|b+)a`)\n"
"['aaa', 'aba', 'abba']"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:104
msgid "Segment-by-Segment Matching"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:106
msgid ""
"Regex globbing splits the pattern by the path separator (``/``) and compiles "
"each segment as a separate regular expression. At each directory level, only "
"entries matching the current segment are visited. This prunes the directory "
"tree early and avoids a full recursive walk."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:111
msgid ""
"However, this means that regex features spanning across ``/`` will break "
"because the segments are no longer valid regexes on their own:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:114
msgid ""
"@ # Group split across / — each segment gets an unmatched parenthesis\n"
"@ r`(.*/)*\\w+\\.py`\n"
"re.error: missing ), unterminated subpattern at position 0\n"
"\n"
"@ # Backreference across / — refers to a group in a different segment\n"
"@ r`(\\w+)/(\\1)\\.py`\n"
"re.error: cannot refer to an open group at position 8"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:126
msgid "Formatted Glob Literals"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:128
msgid ""
"Using the ``f`` modifier with either regex or normal globbing makes the glob "
"pattern behave like a formatted string literal. This can be used to "
"substitute variables and other expressions into the glob pattern:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:132
msgid ""
"@ touch a aa aaa aba abba aab aabb abcba\n"
"@ mypattern = 'ab'\n"
"@ print(f`{mypattern[0]}+`)\n"
"['a', 'aa', 'aaa']\n"
"@ print(gf`{mypattern}*`)\n"
"['aba', 'abba', 'abcba']"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:143
msgid "Match Globbing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:145
msgid ""
"The ``m`` modifier enables **match globbing** — a regex glob that returns "
"capture groups instead of full paths. This lets you destructure matched "
"paths directly:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:149
msgid ""
"@ for parent, file in m`.*/(.*)/(.*\\.py)`:\n"
"      print(parent, file)\n"
"src main.py\n"
"src utils.py\n"
"tests test_main.py"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:157
msgid ""
"When the pattern contains capture groups ``()``, each match returns a tuple "
"of the captured strings. Without groups, full paths are returned — same as "
"``r`` glob, but with full-path matching instead of segment-by-segment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:161
msgid "This is useful for extracting path components without manual splitting:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:163
msgid ""
"@ # Find all .png files and get (directory, filename) pairs\n"
"@ pairs = m`images/(.*)/(.*\\.png)`\n"
"@ print(pairs)\n"
"[('icons', 'logo.png'), ('photos', 'cat.png')]\n"
"\n"
"@ # Single group — returns flat list of strings\n"
"@ names = m`src/(.*)\\.py`\n"
"@ print(names)\n"
"['main', 'utils']"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:175
msgid ""
"Unlike ``r`` glob which matches segment-by-segment, ``m`` glob applies the "
"regex to the **full path**. This means regex features like groups and "
"backreferences can span across ``/``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:179
msgid ""
"``$DOTGLOB`` is respected — hidden files and directories are excluded by "
"default."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:184
msgid "Sorting"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:186
msgid ""
"By default, glob results are sorted alphabetically. The ``$GLOB_SORTED`` "
"environment variable controls this for normal globs. Setting it to ``False`` "
"returns results in arbitrary filesystem order:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:190
msgid ""
"@ $GLOB_SORTED = False\n"
"@ g`*`\n"
"['setup.py', 'README.md']"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:196
msgid "Regex globs are always sorted regardless of this setting."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:200
msgid "XonshList"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:202
msgid ""
"All glob forms (``g``, ``r``, ``m``, ``p``) return a ``XonshList`` — an "
"extended list with convenience methods for common shell operations. Every "
"method returns a new ``XonshList``, so calls can be chained:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:206
msgid ""
"@ g`**/*.py`.files().sorted()\n"
"['setup.py', 'src/main.py', 'tests/test_main.py']\n"
"\n"
"@ r`.*\\.log`.exists().paths()\n"
"[PosixPath('app.log'), PosixPath('error.log')]\n"
"\n"
"@ m`src/(.*)\\.py`.unique().sorted()\n"
"['config', 'main', 'utils']"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:217
msgid "Available methods:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:223
msgid "Method"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:224
msgid "Description"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:225
msgid "``.sorted(key=, reverse=)``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:226
msgid ""
"Return a new sorted list. Unlike ``list.sort()``, returns the list for "
"chaining."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:228
msgid "``.unique()``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:229
msgid "Remove duplicates, preserving order."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:230
msgid "``.filter(func)``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:231
msgid "Keep only elements where ``func(elem)`` is truthy."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:232
msgid "``.select(n)``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:233
msgid ""
"Pick the n-th element from each tuple (for multi-group ``m`` globs). Skips "
"``None`` values from optional groups."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:235
msgid "``.paths()``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:236
msgid "Convert elements to ``pathlib.Path`` objects."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:237
msgid "``.files()``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:238
msgid "Keep only existing regular files."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:239
msgid "``.dirs()``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:240
msgid "Keep only existing directories."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:241
msgid "``.exists()``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:242
msgid "Keep only paths that exist on disk."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:243
msgid "``.visible()``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:244
msgid "Keep only visible (non-hidden) entries."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:245
msgid "``.hidden()``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:246
msgid ""
"Keep only hidden entries (dotfiles on Unix, ``FILE_ATTRIBUTE_HIDDEN`` on "
"Windows)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:249
msgid ""
"A more complete example — find all Python test files, convert to ``Path`` "
"objects, and extract just the filenames:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:252
msgid ""
"@ g`tests/**/*.py`.files().paths().filter(lambda p: p.stem."
"startswith('test_'))\n"
"[PosixPath('tests/test_main.py'), PosixPath('tests/test_utils.py')]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:258
msgid "Working with Tuples from Match Globs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:260
msgid ""
"When ``m`` glob has multiple capture groups, the result is a list of tuples. "
"Path-based methods (``.files()``, ``.dirs()``, ``.exists()``, ``.paths()``) "
"don't work on tuples — use ``.select(n)`` first to extract a specific "
"element:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:264
msgid ""
"@ results = m`src/(.*)/(.*\\.py)`\n"
"@ print(results[:2])\n"
"[('lib', 'utils.py'), ('lib', 'main.py')]\n"
"\n"
"@ results.files()  # TypeError!\n"
"TypeError: .files() requires string paths, got tuples.\n"
"Use .select(n) to pick a tuple element first, e.g. .select(0).files()\n"
"\n"
"@ # Extract directory names\n"
"@ results.select(0).unique().sorted()\n"
"['lib', 'tests']\n"
"\n"
"@ # Extract filenames\n"
"@ results.select(1).sorted()\n"
"['main.py', 'utils.py']"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:282
msgid ""
"Methods that don't need paths — ``.unique()``, ``.sorted()``, ``.filter()`` "
"— work on tuples directly:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:285
msgid ""
"@ m`src/(.*)/(.*\\.py)`.unique().sorted()[:3]\n"
"[('lib', 'main.py'), ('lib', 'utils.py'), ('tests', 'test_main.py')]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:290
msgid ""
"``XonshList`` is a regular ``list`` subclass, so it works everywhere a list "
"does — iteration, indexing, ``len()``, passing to functions, etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:295
msgid "Full-Path Regex via Custom Search"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:297
msgid ""
"Xonsh supports custom path search functions with the ``@func`pattern``` "
"syntax. This can be used to implement full-path regex matching where the "
"pattern is applied to the entire path, not split by ``/``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:301
msgid ""
"@ def refullglob(pattern):\n"
"      import re, os\n"
"       regex = re.compile(pattern)\n"
"       results = []\n"
"       for root, dirs, files in os.walk('.'):\n"
"           for name in dirs + files:\n"
"               path = os.path.join(root, name)\n"
"               if regex.fullmatch(path):\n"
"                   results.append(path)\n"
"       return results\n"
"@ @refullglob`(.*/)*\\w+\\.py`\n"
"['./src/main.py', './src/utils.py', './tests/test_main.py']"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:316
msgid "This approach has trade-offs compared to the built-in ``r`` glob:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:318
msgid ""
"**Pro:** the full ``re`` syntax works — groups, backreferences, and "
"alternations can span across ``/``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:320
msgid ""
"**Con:** it always walks the entire directory tree. On large trees this is "
"slower than the built-in segment-by-segment pruning."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../globbing.rst:322
msgid ""
"**Con:** ``$DOTGLOB`` is not automatically respected — add filtering for "
"names starting with ``.`` if needed."
msgstr ""
